  <heading level="2">Multiply Rational Expressions</heading>
  <paragraph>To multiply rational expressions, we do just what we did with numerical fractions. We multiply the numerators and multiply the denominators. Then, if there are any common factors, we remove them to simplify the result.</paragraph>
  <note type="rule" source_type="Multiplication of Rational Expressions" title="Multiplication of Rational Expressions">
    <paragraph>If $$p,q,r,s$$ are polynomials where $$q \neq 0 \text{and} s \neq 0$$ , then</paragraph>
    <paragraph math="block">$$\frac{p}{q} \cdot \frac{r}{s} = \frac{pr}{qs}$$</paragraph>
    <paragraph>To multiply rational expressions, multiply the numerators and multiply the denominators.</paragraph>
  </note>
  <paragraph>We’ll do the first example with numerical fractions to remind us of how we multiplied fractions without variables.</paragraph>
  <example number="8.17">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Multiply: $$\frac{10}{28} \cdot \frac{8}{15}.$$</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Multiply the numerators and denominators.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Look for common factors, and then remove them.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </solution>
  </example>
  <note type="practice" source_type="Try It" title="Try It" number="8.33">
