  <paragraph>Remember, throughout this chapter, we will assume that all numerical values that would make the denominator be zero are excluded. We will not write the restrictions for each rational expression, but keep in mind that the denominator can never be zero. So in this next example, $$x \neq 0$$ and $$y \neq 0$$ .</paragraph>
  <example number="8.18">
    <problem>
      <paragraph>Mulitply: $$\frac{2x}{3y^{2}} \cdot \frac{6xy^{3}}{x^{2}y}.$$</paragraph>
    </problem>
    <solution>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <cell/>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Multiply.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Factor the numerator and denominator completely, and then remove common factors.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Simplify.</cell>
            <cell/>
          </row>
